        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Text: Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the concept of English as a global language to classmates, using relatable visuals like a world map to show countries where English is spoken (e.g., "English has become an international language, widely used in business to help people communicate.")<br>Can summarize instructions for an English language workshop, using gestures to highlight key session goals (e.g., points to icons of travel and work, and says, "We'll practice English skills for work and travel situations.")<br>Can clarify the importance of English in international communication, using bilingual support and visuals as needed to convey the message (e.g., shows symbols of different career fields and explains, "Knowing English can help in global careers like business and diplomacy.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map highlighting countries where English is widely used in business and diplomatic contexts. Students briefly discuss in pairs what they observe. The teacher then asks students to *explain* why English *has become* such a vital *lingua franca* for *global interactions* and *workplaces*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to *explain* a complex concept simply. For example, the teacher says, "Imagine someone asks you, 'What is *cross-cultural communication*?' You *could* explain it like this: 'It's about *learning* to *communicate* effectively with people from different *backgrounds* and understanding their ways.'" The teacher models the long /e/ sound in "relevant" and "necessary" and the pronunciation of "communication" /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I need to *explain* the purpose of our blog project, *should* I use very technical terms or simple words?" (Simple words)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I *summarize* the instructions for a task, do I include every single detail, or just the main, *relevant* points?" (Just the main points)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a projected image of a short online announcement for an "International Communication Workshop: *Enhancing* Your English *Skills* for *Global Workplaces*." The announcement includes sections on workshop goals, schedule, and key topics such as "Cross-cultural Communication," "Effective *Business* English," and "Using *Online Platforms* for *Global* Collaboration."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Text: Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist:</b> "What is the primary objective of this workshop?" (To help *employees* *enhance* their *communication skills* for *global workplaces*.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match workshop session titles (e.g., "Using *Online Platforms* for *Global* Collaboration") with their brief descriptions or expected outcomes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students determine if statements about the workshop's content or benefits are true or false (e.g., "The workshop focuses only on written *communication*." -> False).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present perfect, modals ("must," "could," "should," "can"), indirect questions. Vocabulary: *English, workplaces, communication, skills, fluency, resources, business, diplomacy, lingua franca, backgrounds, cross-cultural communication, global, interactions, accessible*. Pronunciation: Long vowel sound /e/ (relevant, necessary), full/reduced vowels (communication, accessible).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences that either *explain* a concept or *summarize* a point about English's global role or the workshop.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"English _________ (become) an *international language*, widely used in *business*." (has become)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To succeed *globally*, you _________ (must/should) *enhance* your *communication skills*." (must/should)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A *lingua franca* is a common language that people from different *backgrounds* __________ (use) to connect." (use)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher monitors for correct grammar and pronunciation of words like "relevant."
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Guided Explanation/Summarization):</b> In pairs, students receive short cards with either a term (e.g., "*Diplomacy*," "*Global Business*") or a brief instruction (e.g., "Prepare for a job interview in English"). Student A reads the card, and Student B *explains* the term in simpler terms or *summarizes* the instruction. They then switch roles.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompt: "Explain the term '*cross-cultural communication*'." Student B: "It *could* be explained as understanding how different cultures *communicate* and adapting your style."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompt: "Summarize: 'The blog post *must* be between 250-300 words and include at least two examples.'" Student B: "We *must write* 250-300 words for the blog, and it *should* have two examples."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students are encouraged to ask indirect questions: "Can you *explain* what *fluency* means in this *context*?"
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students are given a brief, new set of instructions for the "Global English Blog" project (e.g., "All sources *must* be cited," "Consider your audience for *formal* language"). In pairs, they take turns *summarizing* the instructions to their partner, making sure to use modals where *necessary*. Afterwards, they *explain* one *relevant* concept from the blog project (e.g., "What makes a blog post *accessible* to a *global* audience?"). Peers provide feedback on clarity and accurate use of target language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Global English Blog Project Presentation & Clarification Session"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups to prepare a short, informal presentation (5 minutes) for another group of "new students" (their peers) who are joining the "Global English Blog" project late. Their task is to act as mentors and *explain* the project clearly and *summarize* the main instructions, ensuring the new students understand their tasks. They *must*:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Explain* the overall concept: "Our blog *has become* a platform to *explore* how *English is used* in diverse *workplaces globally*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Summarize* the main instructions for writing a blog post: "First, you *must choose* a topic related to *international communication*. Then, you *should* use the *present perfect* to discuss how *English has spread*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Clarify* any key terms or concepts: "Can you *explain* what we mean by a '*lingua franca*'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use visuals (a simple blog outline, a world map).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answer indirect questions from the "new students": "Can you *tell* me which *resources could* help me *access* information?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher observes groups, encouraging the use of *formal language* where *relevant* for explaining project guidelines and adapting language for *cross-cultural communication needs*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and use of target language. Peer assessment using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Global English Blog Project Presentation & Clarification Session" for another group. Peers use a checklist to evaluate the clarity and effectiveness of their classmates' *explanations* and *summaries*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Can students *explain* general concepts related to the blog (e.g., *global, communication, cross-cultural*) in simple, understandable terms?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of Summarization:</b> Can students *summarize* the blog project instructions accurately, highlighting key goals and requirements (e.g., word count, use of present perfect)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Effective use of present perfect (e.g., "English *has become*"), modals (*must, could, should, can*), and indirect questions (e.g., "Can you *explain* how...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of *global, international, workplaces, communication, skills, fluency, business, diplomacy, lingua franca, backgrounds*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with an attempt at the long /e/ sound in words like "*relevant*" and clear articulation of multisyllabic words like "*communication*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Pragmatics:</b> Engages effectively with group members and peers during Q&A, using *formal language* in *workplace contexts* and adapting for *cross-cultural communication needs*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How clearly *could* I *explain* the main goals of our 'Global English Blog' project to my peers?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one instruction for the blog I *summarized* effectively today, and what *could* I *improve* for my next presentation?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why *has* it *become* important to *explain* ideas clearly and *summarize* information effectively in English for a *global* audience?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what future *workplace contexts* *could* I use the *mediation skills* (explaining, summarizing, clarifying) practiced today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students *were able to explain* the blog project concept and *summarize* key instructions effectively. The visuals greatly helped in *mediating* complex ideas. Some students still struggle with distinguishing between full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like "*accessible*." For future *interactions*, I *should* incorporate more targeted pronunciation drills for specific words. The peer assessment provided good insights into areas for improvement in *clarity* and *formal language* use. The use of indirect questions *could* be further *enhanced*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "How English Is Used in Workplaces Globally" and serves as a preparation for the final submission of the "Global English Blog" project. The *mediation skills* developed today are *vital* for ensuring their blog posts are *accessible* and clearly *communicated* to diverse *global* readers, and for future *cross-cultural communication* scenarios.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>